  same things this smaller machine can  only make smaller things it is less  stiff and therefore can make things that  are less precise and so it's kind of  reimagining of what programming material  is like or what it is like to fabricate  stuff making everything just smaller and  cheaper is not really the direction that  maybe we should be going in if you look  at why this is the case you know as  people develop digital fabrication as we  know it now CAD tools cam tools and  machinists that would execute those  programs those are all different roles  those are all people you know we're  programming programming things but  things do not just program themselves  and your introduction Schuyler you said  that I make machines that make machines  is not true I just make machines that  make stuff Lea and people that program  those machines are who decides what  stuff gets made and when you program a  machine now you enact all of the  different roles of these peoples or  these jobs and careers that have taken  place and are really historically  grounded at the time you needed someone  else who is going to be running a  computer because like Nicholas mentioned  those things were 250,000 dollars and  someone else is running the milling  machine because also similarly expensive  and now all of those things are easier  more accessible and kind of collapsing  and yet the fields that that they embody  remains sort of separate I can program  geometry and Rhyno I can program sensor  networks with LabVIEW and yet if you  wanted to easily flow from one data flow  graph to the next there aren't  necessarily a whole lot of easy  connection points between the two so in  reimagining what maybe digital  fabrication could look like together  with a lot more I thought maybe instead  of mainframes what we need is laptops  you know let's make things portable this  is a this is a hop up digital  fabrication  machine that you can bring around and 3d  printer and a milling machine and a  cutting machine and for some reason I  take it on airplanes  the TSA never asks me questions about it  strange and they ask me questions about  my mouth a lot you know my ass my 3d  mouth but not about this machine and it  fits downstairs downstairs yes  downstairs so if you wanted to come see  it and show you how we made it and what  it does but it doesn't actually make  things that are much more exciting than  this small plastic fish  I can make circuit boards the circuit  boards can run the separate motors of  the machine but it doesn't really extend  outside of the work envelope of this  suitcase and so even though it's  portable it doesn't necessarily work on  its environment you can just take it  with you and it becomes something that  you can do what you otherwise would do  in a large lab with and that was a kind  of frustrating exercise as well to  actually make this all fit into one  briefcase to be able to control both the  3d print head and a milling head you  need different electronics different  electronics and have to be added to this  different board at some point I wanted  it to also pipette so do liquid handling  for bio experiments and then to augment  that board to be able to do other kinds  of control system things was very  frustrating is to redesign the whole  thing and then change things and so we  decided it would be easier if machines  were controlled through networks of  control nodes rather than a single  control unit and historically this has  not been the case because the bandwidth  of communication between nodes on a  network wasn't high enough to be able to  do things like moving around really fast  but now that is no longer the case and  so doing network controls for machines  is really quite feasible and to not stop  there we saw that well why do we  necessarily also have to have this  single work envelope if I want to make  something that's really thin and long  versus something that's really small or  something that's really big why can't I  just change the machine on the fly to  accommodate that and so these are a  reconfigurable modular machine  parts that you then assemble into  machines as opposed to seeing here's the  Star Trek replicator it can do  everything for everyone we're not going  to tell you how it was made because we  don't know either  and so each one of these are nodes  mechanical nodes correspond sort  electrical node which set in software  also corresponds to a virtual machine or  what we have confusingly called a  commercial machine and then there's some  software application that needs to  program it all or the interface and here  is a version of the machine that we  assembled to do film cuttings this is  for access control and very useful to  have for access control if you wanted to  make an object that says hell on one  side or ya on the other or circle on one  side of the square on the other and so  here you can see a network node and a  way that we're actually running this  machine though is still totally separate  where we have a design file which then  creates a tool path which is then being  run on the machine so it's still  enacting the same hierarchy of things  that happen one after another and this  is how it was programmed it creates like  a file it's not necessarily exactly the  same code that's running to Cincinnati  hydrate hell it's not G code but it's  still very very similar and that's maybe  not the way in which workflow  composition should take place if I  wanted to say for example here is a  petri dish when it turns pink please  feed up to this temperature and then  shake it around a bunch how am I going  to specify that in that kind of  programming language they haven't really  kept up and so this is a browser-based  environment for doing workflow  composition for digital fabrication and  also other kinds of processes where  instead of saying okay I'm going to make  one file that is then going to go from  my computer to the computer that is  inevitably attached to these digital  fabrication machines  and run it you can say okay we can  actually now we even have the bandwidth  that we can do closed-loop control from  within what you would otherwise consider  the master computer or the computer that  is trying to generate the instructions  for the machines and so instead of a  kind of classical you have some kind of  file you know you can add a video input  and have your machine respond to the  environment that way or add different  kinds of components and the reason that  we're doing this all in the browser is  because the idea is that this is going  to be something that's not done by  people like me but it's done by people  like kids or people who are trying to  automate something in a different kind  of space and so here you can quickly  create tool paths in your face very  useful and I guess doing all of this we  made different machines for different  applications this was a airfoil that we  made for colleagues in the lab you know  how do you where do you find a six-foot  wide foam cutter the last moment well  you could also just make one or this is  a work that we're doing with Jeff Koons  studio where he constantly needs to  measure all kinds of crazy things and  build all kinds of crazy things and so  if you need to take eight thousand  photographs of a single object it's a  rather tedious task to do by hand so can  you have people working in the studio  automate those things with machines like  this as well but it's not so useful  necessarily for only me to have access  to these tools and and you know the  concept of modularity and machines and  modularity and construction of machines  are not rapid prototyping but rapid  prototyping of rapid prototyping or  rapidly automating things like how do  you lower the threshold from where you  would otherwise say this is something  you need to do by hand - this is  something that you can automate and so  we made a version of this machine kit  out of cardboard  and the 